<h2>How the question arose</h2>
<p>The writ court at Allahabad had allowed a petition by candidates for the post of Assistant Prosecution Officer, quashing a 2020 Office Memorandum and directing that their candidature be considered. The officer concerned rejected the candidature afresh and filed a compliance affidavit in the contempt proceedings that followed. By an order of 3 September 2026, a learned Single Judge rejected that affidavit, held that the State remained obliged to act on the writ directions, required the officer to appear in person and indicated that charges could follow.</p>
<p>The officer&rsquo;s difficulty was procedural as much as substantive. If he treated the contempt order as one passed in criminal jurisdiction, the intra-court Special Appeal under Chapter VIII Rule 5 of the Allahabad High Court Rules appeared barred; his remedies seemed confined to a fresh writ petition or Article 136. It is this fork in the road that the Supreme Court used to examine the architecture of intra-court appeals.</p>

<h2>What the Court held</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Article 226 is one jurisdiction</h4><p>The power under Article 226 is a single, original, special and extraordinary jurisdiction. Labels of &ldquo;civil&rdquo; and &ldquo;criminal&rdquo; are administrative conveniences for listing; they cannot define or divide the jurisdiction itself.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Civil contempt is sui generis</h4><p>Proceedings for civil contempt are not an exercise of criminal jurisdiction. Precedents treating them as such for the purpose of barring appeals were doubted in light of later authority.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Merit-deciding orders are appealable</h4><p>Applying the Chunilal Nanda line, a contempt order that decides an issue of merit or affects rights — including one rejecting a compliance affidavit and compelling compliance over again — can ground an intra-court appeal, subject to the High Court&rsquo;s rules.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The disagreement with Ram Kishan Fauji</h2>
<p>In <em>Ram Kishan Fauji</em> (2017), the Supreme Court had held that whether a Letters Patent appeal lies is determined by the nature of the jurisdiction invoked, traced through the subject matter of the proceedings. The present bench identified a troubling asymmetry in that approach: an unsuccessful litigant in a land or tax writ gets a second look within the High Court, while a person whose personal liberty under Article 21 is at stake may find the intra-court door closed because the underlying matter is labelled criminal. Linking the point to access to justice under Articles 14 and 21, and to the principle that appeal provisions receive an expansive construction where the language permits, the Court framed specific questions and directed the Registry to place the opinion before the Chief Justice of India for constitution of an appropriate bench.</p>

<h2>The operative directions</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Special Appeal permitted.</strong> The appellant was allowed to file an intra-court Special Appeal within a fortnight, with the merits of the contempt order left entirely open.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Contempt stayed.</strong> The contempt proceedings before the Single Judge were stayed for two months.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Reference pending.</strong> The questions on Ram Kishan Fauji await a larger bench; no final view on its correctness was expressed beyond respectful disagreement.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why it matters in practice</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Check the High Court&rsquo;s rules first.</strong> Intra-court appeals are creatures of Letters Patent and local rules; the Delhi High Court position on LPAs differs from Allahabad&rsquo;s Special Appeals, so the maintainability analysis is always rule-specific.</li>
    <li><strong>Contempt orders are not all equal.</strong> Purely procedural directions remain non-appealable; it is the order deciding merits or affecting rights that opens the appellate route.</li>
    <li><strong>Watch the reference.</strong> If a larger bench revisits Ram Kishan Fauji, the maintainability of intra-court appeals in writs arising from criminal subject matter — a recurring question in service, PMLA and quashing-adjacent litigation — could change significantly.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>For litigants before the High Court of Delhi, the immediate takeaway is modest but useful: a contempt order that in substance adjudicates rights may be tested within the High Court itself, and the broader classification debate is now squarely before a larger bench of the Supreme Court.</p>
<div class="note"><p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice. The judgment should be read in full before being relied upon in any proceeding.</p></div>
